WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.100
- One of the skills

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.300
that you need to pick up very quickly

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.180
as a consultant is project management.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.013
As a consultant,

00:00:07.013 --> 00:00:09.970
you regularly work in large organizations

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:14.040
on topics, covering many
functions and business units.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.720
It is the responsibility
of the project lead

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.840
in the consulting team to set
up a governance structure,

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.960
to set up some project
management best practices,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.930
to make sure that the
whole team stays organized

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.510
and can deliver on the things

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.980
that the team needs to deliver on.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.380
Hi guys, welcome to another coffee break

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.630
here on my channel Firm Learning.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.430
My name is Heinrich and on
this channel I want to help you

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:37.710
to become successful.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.140
In the first years of your career.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.060
In today's video,

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.030
I'm going to show you in detail,

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:45.120
how at MBB consulting firms,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.150
the consultants keep the
whole project organized,

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.650
how they organize their shared drives,

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:54.200
how they archive and make files
available to the whole team.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.780
Before we jump in to the video,

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:57.240
I wanna take an opportunity

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.000
to say thank you to all the
sponsors of this channel.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.900
You're really helping me to
bring this channel alive.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.800
This is much appreciated.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.060
If you wanna become a member as well

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:10.030
and get access to some great perks,

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.520
sign up via the join button below.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.550
There's also a link in
the video description.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.980
It would mean the world to me,

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.720
if you become a member of this channel,

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.770
but now let's jump into the topic.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.555
And how do consultants stay organized.

00:01:22.555 --> 00:01:24.230
Consulting firms usually use

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:27.700
some type of shared
drive service provider.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.390
So whether it's Box, Dropbox, OneDrive

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:31.480
or anything related,

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.540
there's going to be a platform

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:36.720
where all your files for a
project are going to stay.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.440
So let's start with
the overarching folder.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:40.960
How do you name it?

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.610
And here a beginner
mistake that some people do

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:47.260
is that they name the folder
the same as the client's name.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.580
So they give the folder
the name of the company

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:50.940
that you are currently working for.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.020
Here the big risks that you want to avoid

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:55.050
is that at some point
you are sitting together

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.450
in a meeting with clients.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:57.830
Now your screen is shared

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.720
and you need to pull out a file

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.300
and now your Explorer pops up.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.650
And now there's a list of all the folders

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.270
with all that your client names

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:06.620
that you're currently working for

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:07.830
or you worked for in the past.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:11.070
Of course, this would be a
major breach of confidentiality.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.520
You do not reveal your client
names to other companies.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:16.530
So make sure that your client folders

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:18.490
for your overarching folder structure

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:21.030
are not named in the company way

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.310
but give it some kind
of a more cryptic name,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.080
somewhat corrupting abbreviation
that you use internally

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.100
so that if your Explorer window pops up

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.210
in front of a client,

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.900
that all your client
names are not revealed.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.230
So how do you organize and
structure the next level of data

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.230
within your folder?

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.730
And now I'm going to show
you a typical structure

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.260
and I used variations of that many times.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.470
Of course, the details
can always depend a bit

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.410
on the needs of the individual projects.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:45.600
But if you follow this,

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.850
usually this would be already
a very solid structure.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:49.683
So first of all,

00:02:49.683 --> 00:02:52.380
you will see that on the
left of each sub folder,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.010
you use numbers to keep
them in the right order.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.340
If you just give them a name,

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.580
then they will usually be
ordered alphabetically.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:02:59.810
And then this can be a mess

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.730
because often of course the best order

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.690
is not the alphabet but
another order that you define.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.230
So give numbers and use
at least two digits,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.910
because often you will
have more than 10 folders.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.050
So use numbers to make sure
that the folder always stay

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.890
in the order that you want them to stay.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.550
So what would be typical folders

00:03:16.550 --> 00:03:18.870
that you'd like to have
on this first level?

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.700
So often I'd like to start
with an admin folder.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:22.560
So this is the folder

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.350
where you put all kinds
of admin documents.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.270
And so for instance,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.410
time sheets for your team,
maybe some other overview,

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:31.020
so that everybody needs access to,

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.410
some travel information,
just admin documents,

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.890
where everybody needs to
have an easy access on it.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:38.930
Next you set up two different
types of meeting folders,

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:42.410
one for internal meetings
and one for client meetings.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.370
So in the internal meeting folder

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.660
you then archive all the
meetings that you have

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.400
with your partner, with your
team, all the internal things,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.300
and then the client
folder as the name says,

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.460
all the interactions where
you're gonna have meetings

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.320
with your clients.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.360
Next you will have a folder
for the initial data requests

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.990
and here not on every project,

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.650
but in many projects
before you actually start,

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.920
you will submit a list of
data that you ask the client

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.950
to prepare to make sure
that once you're there,

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.420
once you're working on the
client that you already have,

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.440
at least most of the things you need

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.120
to enable you to hit the ground running.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.440
This is often important base data

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.230
that the whole team needs access to.

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:19.350
So you make sure that you
have this neatly collected

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.580
in this overarching folder.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.310
Next you will have a folder each

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:25.390
for the key work streams
or modules of your project.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.530
Usually when you set up a project,

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:30.190
you divide it into different subs streams,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.690
into different modules,
into different work streams.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:35.140
And then often you assign each associate

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.620
to one of these modules.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.090
In this specific example,

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:41.330
you work for different
business units of the clients.

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:42.950
So for an energy business unit,

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:44.500
for an automotive business unit

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.670
and for an advanced
industries business unit,

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:47.890
these folders will be owned

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.840
by the associates of your team working

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.180
on the individual modules,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.210
and this is where all the module specific

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.810
where all the work stream
specific content sits

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:56.660
for your project.

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.040
Next you will have some folders

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.720
for the big over arching analysis

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.830
that you are doing for some
work stream overarching,

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.060
some module overarching topics
that your team is working on.

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.270
In this specific example,

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.090
there was an employee survey

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:13.100
where apparently the employees
of the clients were surveyed.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.440
In this folder you could
now organize the survey

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.660
and where you could then
save all the survey results.

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:19.070
In addition it that seem to have happened

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.670
an overarching benchmark
where different functions

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:23.330
of the client were benchmarked.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.250
And again, then you can
set up a folder like this

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:26.190
where you organize it.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:29.750
Next you have a folder with
input from your research team.

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:30.583
And first of all,

00:05:30.583 --> 00:05:33.370
notice on all the first number
of this folder is a nine.

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:34.360
So by giving it a nine,

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:37.020
you make sure that it
always stays at the bottom

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:38.130
of your folder structure.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:38.963
So you do this

00:05:38.963 --> 00:05:41.300
to keep these a bit less important folders

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.900
always at the bottom to not
mess up the whole order.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:45.840
So in such a research input folder

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.510
you can collect all the inputs

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:49.130
that you get from your research team.

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:50.640
So many large consulting firms,

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:51.960
you will have back offices

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.680
that help you to do some research.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.130
Such a folder would be the place

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:56.220
where you organize all this.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:58.520
Then you might have a
folder for old documents,

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.370
maybe there was already a
project going on before you,

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:03.080
and now you just take all the data

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:04.370
and just put it in one folder.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:05.490
So you have it available

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:07.460
if you still need needed at
some point in the future.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:09.360
And then finally you
would often use a folder

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.790
for finding the deliverables

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.440
where you then over time organize

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:13.970
or the final documents

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:15.820
that you would hand over to the client

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.210
at the very end of your project.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:20.160
So lets now go one level
deeper on the meeting folders,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.830
because these are indeed some folders

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:23.420
that are usually heavily used,

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.830
and it's quite important
to have a solid order here.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.730
The first thought is here
that every meeting you have

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:30.730
should be documented in these folders.

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:31.710
So if you wonder, okay,

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.110
what document did I show two weeks ago?

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.800
When I sat together with this guy,

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:37.510
you should always be able to go back

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:38.630
in your folder structure

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.400
and find the very specific document

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.022
that then you wanna pull out.

00:06:42.022 --> 00:06:42.855
And what I learned,

00:06:42.855 --> 00:06:43.720
and what I can really recommend you

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:46.390
is to always then make the folders start

00:06:46.390 --> 00:06:49.110
with the date when the
meeting actually took place.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.710
And here again, make sure
that you start with the year,

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.420
then the month, and then the day,

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.093
because this makes sure
that if you order them

00:06:55.093 --> 00:06:58.080
that the meetings will always
show up chronologically

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.440
as they actually took place.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:00.660
And then often it makes sense to record

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.410
what type of meeting it is.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:04.620
And often also with whom
you had the meetings.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.110
For the internal meeting
folder this can for instance,

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.230
be a team kickoff, the team offsite,

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:11.970
maybe some meetings or calls
you had with partners of yours

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.240
as this list of folders can easily grow

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.980
and can get quite large at some point,

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:17.050
then it can often make sense

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:19.760
than just to put together
all the meetings in one month

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.080
or a monthly folder.

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:21.913
So at some point

00:07:21.913 --> 00:07:23.360
you can just drag all
the meetings in July,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.500
into a July folder

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.547
all the meetings in August
and into an August folder.

00:07:26.547 --> 00:07:28.710
And then you just have
all the detailed list

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:29.770
just for the current month.

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:30.830
Where then you might need

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.430
to more granually log into the meetings.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:33.930
For the clients it's very similar.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:35.780
You might typically have draw fixes.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:36.870
You might have steer cross.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:38.770
You might have other
client meetings like that.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:40.050
If you're interested to learn more,

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.660
of how such a typical work week

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.090
in the life of an MBB
consultant looks like

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:45.160
also check out the video

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.770
that I will insert here somewhere
about to better understand

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.500
how exactly these meetings would play out

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:50.760
during a typical work week.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.420
Now, another little best practice tip

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:53.370
that I wanna give you,

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:55.410
is now to organize the documents

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.230
within these specific meeting folders.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.190
So if you worked together on
a team on such a document of

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:03.080
and then this folder can
easily look like this, right?

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.210
So how a lot of versions

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:07.010
and often if you work on such documents

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.490
like the big steer
documents over some time

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:10.323
with your team,

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:13.170
the list of documents can easily
look like a mess like this.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.100
So with lots of different versions,

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:16.700
lots of different situations.

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:18.900
And at some point it
can get super difficult

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:20.760
for somebody looking at it to understand

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:22.980
what now really the latest version is

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.570
now especially if you put yourself

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.610
into the shoes of for instance, a partner,

00:08:26.610 --> 00:08:28.400
he just wants to access the drive,

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:29.860
just go into the meeting folder

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.210
and just quickly pull
out the latest version

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.630
of the steer document that
actually was shown, right?

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:35.820
Just to look something up.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.680
And for him now if he
sees something like this,

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:38.980
this is really a nightmare

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:41.650
because he doesn't know what
now the latest version was,

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.927
and he pretty much needs to
always call up the team and ask,

00:08:43.927 --> 00:08:45.860
"Hey guys, and what is
not the latest version.

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.750
I don't understand
anything at all anymore.

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:49.290
So here it's a best practice then

00:08:49.290 --> 00:08:51.210
to have such an old folder,

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.650
but then you drag all
the older versions in

00:08:53.650 --> 00:08:55.557
and make sure that in the meetings folder

00:08:55.557 --> 00:08:58.530
you really only have the latest
version of your documents,

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:00.380
the latest, and then
hopefully final version

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:01.370
that then everybody knows,

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:03.980
okay, this is really the
document that we need to look at.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.640
Another little best practice is also

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:07.770
to clearly label the documents

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:09.230
by the status of the release.

00:09:09.230 --> 00:09:11.980
So if you send out a
document to the client

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:14.590
and distinctly save it as a V sent

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:15.660
or something related

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.240
you can present the document,

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:19.710
distinctly save it as a V presented.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:21.830
Or there may be a few
make changes afterwards,

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:24.250
save it as a V final,
but this will enable you,

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:25.120
if they really wanna know, okay,

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:27.360
what was the version that I sent out?

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.690
What was the version
that I really presented?

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:31.800
What was the version
with some last changes?

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.470
And maybe even that after the meeting,

00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:36.570
that all of this is always
clearly labeled, clearly saved.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.640
This can really save you
some significant headache

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:39.990
If then later somebody asks you

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.180
about a specific version
that you're just not able

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:43.070
to understand anymore.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:44.770
Which of the version
numbers it was, right?

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:47.280
So clearly always save
it with these labels.

